G={'W':'Writers & poets','A':'Artists, musicians & entertainers','T':'Tyrants & notorious figures','R':'Rulers & conquerors','F':'Freedom fighters & leaders','S':'Thinkers, scientists & artists','X':'Explorers & travellers','V':'Revolutionaries & reformers'}
P=[
("William Shakespeare",1564,"1564–1616","Playwright and poet","England","Hamlet, Macbeth, Romeo and Juliet","Born in Stratford-upon-Avon to a glove-maker, Shakespeare moved to London around 1590 and became an actor, playwright and shareholder in the Lord Chamberlain's Men, who built the Globe Theatre in 1599. He wrote about 39 plays — tragedies (Hamlet, Othello, King Lear, Macbeth), comedies (A Midsummer Night's Dream, Twelfth Night) and histories (Henry V, Richard III) — and 154 sonnets. He coined or popularised hundreds of English words and phrases. His plays, performed under Elizabeth I and James I, are translated into every major language and staged worldwide, including countless Indian adaptations such as Vishal Bhardwaj's Maqbool, Omkara and Haider.","William_Shakespeare","W","Elizabeth I; Tudor dynasty; Renaissance; English; Christianity"),
("Munshi Premchand",1880,"1880–1936","Novelist and short-story writer","Varanasi, India","Godaan, Idgah, Kafan","Born Dhanpat Rai Srivastava in Lamhi near Varanasi, Premchand worked as a schoolteacher and inspector before resigning in 1921 in response to Gandhi's Non-Cooperation call. He wrote first in Urdu (his 1907 story collection Soz-e-Watan was banned and burned by the British) and then Hindi, producing about 300 stories and over a dozen novels. His works — Godaan, Gaban, Nirmala, and stories like Idgah, Kafan and Poos Ki Raat — depicted peasants, caste oppression, poverty, dowry and colonial exploitation with stark realism. He presided over the first Progressive Writers' Association meeting in 1936 and is called 'Upanyas Samrat' (emperor of novels).","Premchand","W","Non-Cooperation Movement; Mahatma Gandhi; British Raj; Hindi–Urdu (Hindustani)"),
("Marilyn Monroe",1926,"1926–1962","Actress and singer","United States","Some Like It Hot, pop-culture icon","Born Norma Jeane Mortenson in Los Angeles, Monroe spent much of her childhood in foster homes and an orphanage. She became Hollywood's biggest star of the 1950s in films like Gentlemen Prefer Blondes, The Seven Year Itch and Some Like It Hot (Golden Globe, 1960). She founded her own production company in 1955 — rare for an actress — to fight studio control, and studied at the Actors Studio. Her struggles with fame, depression and addiction, her marriages to Joe DiMaggio and Arthur Miller, and her death from a barbiturate overdose at 36 made her an enduring icon of glamour and of the costs of celebrity.","Marilyn_Monroe","A","United States; Charlie Chaplin; Cold War"),
("Charlie Chaplin",1889,"1889–1977","Actor, director and composer","Britain and United States","The Tramp; The Great Dictator","Born into poverty in London, Chaplin performed on stage from childhood and went to the US in 1910. His 'Little Tramp' character, with bowler hat, cane and moustache, made him the world's most famous film star in silent films like The Kid (1921), The Gold Rush (1925), City Lights (1931) and Modern Times (1936), which satirised industrial life. In The Great Dictator (1940) he mocked Hitler. Accused of communist sympathies during the McCarthy era, he was barred from re-entering the US in 1952 and settled in Switzerland. He met Gandhi in London in 1931 and discussed machines and labour.","Charlie_Chaplin","A","Adolf Hitler; Great Depression; Industrial Revolution; Mahatma Gandhi; World War II; United States; Cold War"),
("Valmiki",-500,"c. 5th–1st century BCE (traditional)","Poet-sage","India","Ramayana","Valmiki is honoured as the adi kavi ('first poet') of Sanskrit and the author of the Ramayana, the 24,000-verse epic of Rama, Sita, Lakshmana and Hanuman. Legend says he was once a robber named Ratnakara who became a sage after meditating so long that an anthill (valmika) grew over him. The epic, composed over centuries, spread across Asia in countless versions, from Tamil Kamban and Tulsidas's Hindi Ramcharitmanas to Thailand's Ramakien and Indonesia's Kakawin Ramayana.","Valmiki","W","Hinduism; Vedic India; Classical Sanskrit; Tulsidas"),
("Vyasa",-400,"Traditional; text composed c. 400 BCE–400 CE","Sage","India","Mahabharata","Vyasa (Krishna Dvaipayana) is traditionally the compiler of the Vedas and the author of the Mahabharata, the world's longest epic (about 100,000 couplets), which includes the Bhagavad Gita. Legend says Ganesha wrote it down as Vyasa dictated. The epic tells of the war at Kurukshetra between the Pandavas and Kauravas and explores dharma, duty and fate.","Vyasa","W","Hinduism; Kurukshetra and the Mahajanapadas; Classical Sanskrit; Vedic religion"),
("Homer",-750,"c. 8th century BCE","Poet","Ionia, Greece","Iliad and Odyssey","Homer is the legendary blind poet credited with the Iliad, about the wrath of Achilles in the Trojan War, and the Odyssey, about Odysseus's ten-year journey home. The epics grew out of oral tradition and were written down after the Greek alphabet was adopted. They shaped Greek education, religion and identity, and remain foundational works of Western literature.","Homer","W","Trojan War (traditional); Mycenaean Greece; Classical Greece; Greek alphabet; Greek religion and mythology"),
("Tulsidas",1532,"1532–1623","Poet-saint","Varanasi","Ramcharitmanas, Hanuman Chalisa","Tulsidas, a Brahmin devotee of Rama, retold the Ramayana in Awadhi Hindi as the Ramcharitmanas (1574–77), making the epic accessible to ordinary people. He also wrote the Hanuman Chalisa. The Ramlila performances of north India are based on his work, and he lived during Akbar's reign.","Tulsidas","W","Bhakti movement; Hinduism; Mughal Empire; Akbar; Valmiki"),
("Surdas",1478,"c. 1478–1583","Poet-saint","Braj, India","Sur Sagar","Surdas, traditionally said to be blind from birth, composed thousands of devotional songs in Braj Bhasha about the child Krishna and his love for Radha, collected as the Sur Sagar. He belonged to the Vallabhacharya tradition of Krishna devotion.","Surdas","W","Bhakti movement; Hinduism; Mirabai; Mughal Empire"),
("Mirza Ghalib",1797,"1797–1869","Poet","Delhi","Urdu and Persian ghazals","Mirza Asadullah Khan Ghalib was the greatest Urdu poet of the late Mughal court of Bahadur Shah Zafar. His ghazals, full of wit, longing and philosophy, are still widely sung. His letters and diary Dastanbuy describe the destruction of Delhi in 1857.","Mirza_Ghalib","W","Mughal Empire; Indian Rebellion of 1857; Hindi–Urdu (Hindustani); Islam"),
("Allama Iqbal",1877,"1877–1938","Poet and philosopher","Punjab","Saare Jahan Se Achha; idea of Pakistan","Muhammad Iqbal, educated in Lahore, Cambridge and Munich, wrote in Urdu and Persian. His poem 'Saare Jahan Se Achha' (1904) celebrated India, but his later poetry focused on Muslim revival, and his 1930 Allahabad address proposed a Muslim state in northwest India, inspiring Pakistan.","Muhammad_Iqbal","W","British Raj; Indian Independence and Partition; Islam; Sufism"),
("Bankim Chandra Chattopadhyay",1838,"1838–1894","Novelist","Bengal","Anandamath and Vande Mataram","Bankim Chandra, one of the first graduates of Calcutta University and a colonial official, wrote the first major Bengali novels. His Anandamath (1882), set during the Sannyasi rebellion, contained the song 'Vande Mataram', which became a rallying cry of the freedom movement and India's national song.","Bankim_Chandra_Chattopadhyay","W","British Raj; Partition of Bengal; Hinduism; Bengali"),
("Subramania Bharati",1882,"1882–1921","Poet and journalist","Tamil Nadu","Modern Tamil poetry, women's rights","Subramania Bharati, 'Mahakavi Bharati', wrote patriotic poems and essays in Tamil that inspired the freedom movement, and championed women's liberation and an end to caste. Persecuted by the British, he lived in French Pondicherry for a decade.","Subramania_Bharati","W","British Raj; Tamil (Classical); Partition of Bengal"),
("Rumi",1207,"1207–1273","Poet and Sufi mystic","Anatolia","Masnavi","Jalal al-Din Rumi, born in Balkh, fled the Mongols and settled in Konya. His friendship with the wandering mystic Shams of Tabriz transformed him into an ecstatic poet. His Masnavi, 25,000 Persian couplets, is called 'the Quran in Persian', and his followers founded the Mevlevi 'whirling dervishes'.","Rumi","W","Sufism; Islam; Seljuk Empire; Mongol conquests; Classical Persian"),
("Ferdowsi",940,"940–1020","Poet","Khorasan, Iran","Shahnameh","Ferdowsi spent about 30 years writing the Shahnameh ('Book of Kings'), a 50,000-couplet epic of Iran's mythical and historical kings, including the hero Rostam. Written in Persian with few Arabic words, it preserved Persian language and identity.","Ferdowsi","W","Classical Persian; Sasanian Empire; Zoroastrianism; Achaemenid Persian Empire"),
("Dante Alighieri",1265,"1265–1321","Poet","Florence","Divine Comedy","Dante wrote the Divine Comedy, a journey through Hell, Purgatory and Paradise, in Tuscan Italian rather than Latin, helping create the Italian language. He was exiled from Florence for his politics.","Dante_Alighieri","W","Christianity; Renaissance; Latin"),
("Miguel de Cervantes",1547,"1547–1616","Novelist","Spain","Don Quixote","Cervantes, who lost the use of his left hand at the Battle of Lepanto and was a captive in Algiers for five years, wrote Don Quixote (1605, 1615), often called the first modern novel, about a man who imagines himself a knight.","Miguel_de_Cervantes","W","Spanish Empire; Ottoman Empire; Spanish"),
("Leo Tolstoy",1828,"1828–1910","Novelist and moralist","Russia","War and Peace, Anna Karenina","Count Leo Tolstoy wrote War and Peace (about Napoleon's invasion of Russia) and Anna Karenina. Later he embraced Christian pacifism and non-violence; his 'Letter to a Hindu' (1908) and correspondence with Gandhi influenced satyagraha, and Gandhi named Tolstoy Farm in South Africa after him.","Leo_Tolstoy","W","Russian Empire; Napoleonic Wars; Mahatma Gandhi; Christianity; Russian"),
("Fyodor Dostoevsky",1821,"1821–1881","Novelist","Russia","Crime and Punishment","Dostoevsky was sentenced to death for revolutionary activity in 1849, reprieved at the last moment and sent to a Siberian labour camp. His novels Crime and Punishment, The Idiot and The Brothers Karamazov explore guilt, faith and freedom.","Fyodor_Dostoevsky","W","Russian Empire; Christianity; Russian"),
("Victor Hugo",1802,"1802–1885","Novelist and poet","France","Les Misérables","Victor Hugo wrote The Hunchback of Notre-Dame and Les Misérables, a story of poverty and justice. He opposed Napoleon III and spent 19 years in exile, and campaigned against the death penalty.","Victor_Hugo","W","French Revolution; Revolutions of 1848; French"),
("Charles Dickens",1812,"1812–1870","Novelist","England","Oliver Twist, A Christmas Carol","Dickens, who worked in a blacking factory at 12 when his father was jailed for debt, exposed the poverty of industrial London in Oliver Twist, David Copperfield and A Christmas Carol.","Charles_Dickens","W","Industrial Revolution; British Empire; Queen Victoria; English"),
("Jane Austen",1775,"1775–1817","Novelist","England","Pride and Prejudice","Jane Austen's novels — Pride and Prejudice, Sense and Sensibility, Emma — portrayed women, marriage and class in Georgian England with irony.","Jane_Austen","W","British Empire; English; Napoleonic Wars"),
("Mark Twain",1835,"1835–1910","Writer","United States","Huckleberry Finn","Samuel Clemens, as Mark Twain, wrote The Adventures of Tom Sawyer and Huckleberry Finn, satirising racism along the Mississippi. He visited India in 1896 and wrote about it in Following the Equator.","Mark_Twain","W","United States; American Civil War; Atlantic slave trade; English"),
("Lu Xun",1881,"1881–1936","Writer","China","The True Story of Ah Q","Lu Xun, the father of modern Chinese literature, gave up medicine to write. His 'A Madman's Diary' (1918) and 'The True Story of Ah Q' criticised traditional society.","Lu_Xun","W","Xinhai Revolution; Qing dynasty; Mandarin Chinese"),
("Murasaki Shikibu",973,"c. 973–1014","Novelist","Japan","The Tale of Genji","Murasaki Shikibu, a lady-in-waiting at the Heian court, wrote The Tale of Genji, often called the world's first novel.","Murasaki_Shikibu","W","Heian Japan; Tale of Genji; Japanese"),
("Chinua Achebe",1930,"1930–2013","Novelist","Nigeria","Things Fall Apart","Achebe's Things Fall Apart (1958), about an Igbo leader facing colonialism and missionaries, is the most widely read African novel.","Chinua_Achebe","W","Scramble for Africa; Decolonisation of Africa and Asia; British Empire"),
("Gabriel García Márquez",1927,"1927–2014","Novelist","Colombia","One Hundred Years of Solitude","García Márquez's One Hundred Years of Solitude (1967) made 'magical realism' famous. He won the Nobel Prize in 1982.","Gabriel_Garc%C3%ADa_M%C3%A1rquez","W","Spanish; Latin American independence"),
]
